WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.560
Oh, hi.

9
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.880
Oh, I'm excited for the show today.

10
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.360
I am so tickled about today's episode.

11
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.380
I just loved it so much.

12
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.300
We have a special interview today.

13
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:41.220
It is with our safety meeting queen, first AD of The Office, Kelly Cantley.

14
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:41.700
Yay!

15
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:53.400
We love getting mail from all of you, asking questions about Phil Shea and his props tray, or about standards and practices, or Bob Dunn's animal rentals.

16
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.160
And you know so many of our crew's names.

17
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.380
You know the ins and outs of the show now.

18
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.700
Yes, you have really gotten as invested in the nuts and bolts of the show as we have.

19
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.520
And so, we're so excited for you to hear about the making of The Office from Kelly's perspective.

20
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.680
This interview, oh my gosh, the nuggets.

21
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.180
So many nuggets.

22
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.100
You know, Kelly was the assistant director for 72 episodes.

23
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.820
And she has some stories.

24
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:19.660
Oh, does she?

25
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:20.220
Mm-hmm.

26
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.880
She even has more that we want her to come back and tell.

27
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.560
Well, when we were getting ready to talk to Kelly, I went in my digital clutter just to see what I could find.

28
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.180
And you know what?

29
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.920
I found a very funny email.

30
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.600
I want to share with you first, though, that Kelly Cantley and I are very close in height.

31
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.020
We both have blonde hair.

32
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.180
And from time to time, if we were on set, and they were trying to figure something out with my character and I wasn't there,

33
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.940
they would ask Kelly to stand in.

34
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.920
And this is what I found in my digital clutter.

35
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.500
I can't wait to bring this up to her.

36
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.860
It was from Steve Burgess to me, Kelly, and Phil Shea.

37
00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.800
Titled, Foot Photos.

38
00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.340
November 16, 2012.

39
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:01.940
Steve wrote,

40
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:08.340
We took these photos of Kelly's foot today, wondering if you can contort your foot to look equally as bad.

41
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.400
I think Kelly may call you to talk about this foot gag.

42
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.520
Phil Shea will see you at 2 p.m.

43
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.260
Thanks, Steve.

44
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.460
I responded with this.

45
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.520
I think my foot will do this.

46
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.100
And then Phil Shea wrote,

47
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.220
Okay, great.

48
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.660
So, Tim will photograph your feet at 2 p.m.

49
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.300
Thank you.

50
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.900
What is this?

51
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:33.200
And then there are several photos of Kelly's feet and my feet and us trying to get them to do the same things.

52
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.500
I was so incredibly curious.

53
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.520
I emailed Steve Burgess and asked him,

54
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.660
What was this about?

55
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.460
He shared to me that it was from a runner for customer loyalty that we didn't end up using,

56
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.760
and he included a script page.

57
00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.320
It read like this.

58
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.120
Pam walks to the accounting area with her bag.

59
00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.340
She announces,

60
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.500
Well, I'm heading to Cece's recital.

61
00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:55.960
Oscar goes,

62
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:56.500
Aww,

63
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.300
and smiles at Pam for what he feels like is the appropriate amount of time,

64
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.360
and then drops it and turns back to his computer.

65
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:04.300
Angela says,

66
00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.100
Did I tell you I danced as a child?

67
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.840
On point by age 2.

68
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.080
She's very proud.

69
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.660
And then she says,

70
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.660
It permanently stunted my foot growth.

71
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.560
Kevin says,

72
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.100
It's true.

73
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.820
I can fit her shoe all the way in my mouth.

74
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:19.700
Angela's talking head.

75
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:20.700
She holds up her foot,

76
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.420
which is gnarled and small.

77
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.360
Then the camera was going to reveal my feet,

78
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.840
and Phil Shea would have had to have made a foot prosthetic that was curved and tiny,

79
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.220
and this whole entire storyline was cut out.

80
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.340
Oh.

81
00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.120
My.

82
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:34.900
Gosh.

83
00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:35.380
Yeah.

84
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:36.080
That's amazing.

85
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.180
Well, that's the episode that Kelly directed.

86
00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:38.840
Yes.

87
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.000
In season 9.

88
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.620
Well, I can't wait to talk to her about this and many more things,

89
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.300
and I can't wait for you guys to hear everything she has to share.

90
00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.600
It's absolutely wonderful.

91
00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.200
Well, let's take a break,

92
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.300
and when we come back,

93
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.440
we're going to jump right in.

94
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:52.260
Yay!

95
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.260
Kelly's here!

96
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.520
Hi, Kelly.

97
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.320
How are you?

98
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:06.900
I'm awesome.

99
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:07.680
Hi, Ange.

100
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.120
Hi, Jenna.

101
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.780
It's so great to see you guys.

102
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:11.060
We are so excited.

103
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.100
We are so excited to have you today.

104
00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:12.880
You have no idea.

105
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.680
We talk about you all the time on the podcast,

106
00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.700
which you told us people tell you that we talk about you on the podcast,

107
00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.900
and now we have you here,

108
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.340
and we get to share you with everyone.

109
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.720
We have lots of questions,

110
00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.440
so I'm just going to jump right in with question number one,

111
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.260
which is we always ask our guests how they came to work on The Office,

112
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.120
so can you tell us your story?

113
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.700
I had just turned into a first AD,

114
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:37.780
so I was doing both.

115
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.200
I was doing second AD work and first AD work,

116
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.980
and I had made a decision to only be a first AD

117
00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.620
and not keep working as a second,

118
00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.240
and then Ross Novy called me.

119
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.140
He was doing a pilot called Early Bird

120
00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.160
with Kent Zvornak producing.

121
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.460
So Kent Zvornak was the producer on Early Bird

122
00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.380
and also was the producer, as he described it,

123
00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.460
of a little comedy called The Office,

124
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.000
and I took the job as a second.

125
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.600
Ross told everybody I was doing him a favor,

126
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.780
and I was really a first,

127
00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.660
and a few weeks after the pilot,

128
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.320
Kent Zvornak called me and said,

129
00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.960
hey, this little show I'm doing called The Office.

130
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:16.860
It's really funny.

131
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.100
It's a group of nice people.

132
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.000
Are you available?

133
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.900
Can you come meet the showrunner?

134
00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.140
So I went somewhere in the middle of Burbank

135
00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:25.320
to a very strange,

136
00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.220
I think it was a post-production house

137
00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.200
because Greg was working on season one.

138
00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.100
I went and found Kent,

139
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.340
and we sat in a small room,

140
00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.040
and Greg Daniels came in

141
00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.700
and asked me a bunch of questions

142
00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.480
about what I did in my free time in my real life.

143
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:40.980
We talked about sailing,

144
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.560
and we talked about hiking.

145
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.940
Then he looked over his shoulder and walked out

146
00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.400
after about five, six minutes.

147
00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.330
He was dead silent.

148
00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.170
I look at Kent,

149
00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:50.530
and I said,

150
00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:51.730
do you think he's coming back?

151
00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:52.350
Kent said,

152
00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.630
unknown, let's wait 10 minutes.

153
00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.070
After 10 minutes,

154
00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.250
Greg didn't come back,

155
00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.710
and Kent said,

156
00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.450
well, I guess the interview's over.

157
00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:01.410
I'll give you a call.

158
00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:01.870
I was like,

159
00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:02.830
all right, great,

160
00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.290
and he called me the next day

161
00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:04.730
and said,

162
00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.990
we'd like you to start on season two.

163
00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:08.990
You must have some great sailing

164
00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.270
and hiking stories.

165
00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.330
It's like so random.

166
00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:16.330
What did you think when you got the job?

167
00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:16.830
Were you like,

168
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.290
what have I gotten myself into?

169
00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.250
So I hadn't done a lot of comedy.

170
00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.870
I had done a couple of comedy things.

171
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.350
The pilot was a comedy,

172
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.190
and the pilot that I did,

173
00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.070
we worked less than 12 hours.

174
00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:30.410
Everybody was happy.

175
00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.670
It was funny,

176
00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:32.630
and I thought,

177
00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:33.110
yes,

178
00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:34.470
I'd like to do a comedy,

179
00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:35.890
more of this,

180
00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.590
and I hadn't seen any of season one,

181
00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:40.810
the first six episodes,

182
00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.070
when I did the interview.

183
00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:43.010
Fortunately,

184
00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:44.530
we didn't talk about season one

185
00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:45.330
or television,

186
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:48.190
and then I got the DVD,

187
00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.810
and I watched all the episodes.

188
00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.250
I can't remember if I got the DVD

189
00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.990
or if they re-aired or something on NBC,

190
00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.650
and I watched all the episodes,

191
00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:55.490
and at the end of it,

192
00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:55.910
I thought,

193
00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:56.330
huh,

194
00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:57.370
well,

195
00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.170
the people are nice.

196
00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.110
It's 28 minutes from my house.

197
00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:01.330
We're going to do 12 episodes,

198
00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:02.990
and then I'll be looking for work again.

199
00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:04.670
It wasn't your cup of tea.

200
00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:06.130
No,

201
00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:06.890
I was just like,

202
00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:07.370
huh,

203
00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.330
and then,

204
00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:09.610
I don't,

205
00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:10.090
somewhere,

206
00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:11.890
like four or five episodes

207
00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:12.890
into season two,

208
00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:14.110
it was so funny.

209
00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:14.770
I was like,

210
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:15.750
this is the funniest thing

211
00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:16.410
I've ever seen.

212
00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:17.410
Aw.

213
00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.470
You know,

214
00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:19.790
for our listeners

215
00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:20.950
who maybe aren't familiar

216
00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.630
with exactly what

217
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.190
a first AD does

218
00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.510
for a production,

219
00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:26.490
could you give us

220
00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.650
a little brief description

221
00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:28.330
of your job?

222
00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:29.550
So,

223
00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.590
I used to describe my job

224
00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:32.850
as a cross between

225
00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:33.770
a cocktail waitress

226
00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:35.090
and a fourth grade teacher.

227
00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:36.710
You have time to go

228
00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:37.330
to the bathroom,

229
00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.510
you pick your toys up,

230
00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.890
and get ready to go to lunch.

231
00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.170
But now,

232
00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:42.210
the more grown-up way

233
00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:42.990
to describe it

234
00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:44.590
is a project manager.

235
00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:45.350
Uh-huh.

236
00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:45.630
So,

237
00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:46.870
I take the script,

238
00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.030
which is like the blueprint,

239
00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.030
and I do all the prep

240
00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.250
to make it so that

241
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:51.850
we can shoot

242
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:52.750
what's on the page,

243
00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:53.930
and then when we shoot,

244
00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.090
I run the floor

245
00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:55.890
up by camera.

246
00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:56.650
Kelly,

247
00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:58.170
did you ever get a script

248
00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:58.970
and think,

249
00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.670
how are we going to do this?

250
00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.030
Yes.

251
00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:03.610
A couple of them.

252
00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:04.710
One of them

253
00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:05.810
was the fire drill

254
00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:06.470
called Open.

255
00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:07.210
Mm-hmm.

256
00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:07.750
Uh-huh.

257
00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.190
What part of it?

258
00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.190
There was so many

259
00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.470
crazy things,

260
00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:12.390
which was the one

261
00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.150
that stumped you?

262
00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:13.890
Like,

263
00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:14.470
all of it.

264
00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:14.830
Okay,

265
00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.010
so all of it

266
00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:16.990
was a little crazy

267
00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:17.610
but doable.

268
00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:18.810
There's lots of chaos

269
00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:19.710
and running around

270
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:20.330
and smoke,

271
00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.670
and we had to figure out

272
00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:23.370
how to lock the doors

273
00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.330
but not violate fire code

274
00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.470
so that if something happened,

275
00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:28.150
you guys could

276
00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:29.470
actually get off the set.

277
00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:32.630
And then the cat.

278
00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:34.250
Oh, Lord.

279
00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:34.910
The cat.

280
00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.350
So at some point

281
